  "2ebcd71b-a872-4d63-9039-672a26c20cc5": {
   "id": "2ebcd71b-a872-4d63-9039-672a26c20cc5",
   "message": {
    "id": "2ebcd71b-a872-4d63-9039-672a26c20cc5",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1779930062.229,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "In Ireland, **cash or a cheque in a card** is very common, often handed over at the reception or placed in a card box. Some couples have a \"wedding list\" (registry), and many now include a honeymoon fund link.\n\nTypical amounts reported in Irish surveys and etiquette columns: **€100–200 per guest** for close family or friends, more for very close family; travelling a long way is widely understood to be a cost in itself, so guests coming from abroad sometimes give a little less, and nobody will mind.\n\nPractical tips:\n- Bring euro in a sealed card rather than USD. A bank transfer is also normal if the couple shares details.\n- Write a personal note. It matters more than the amount.\n- RSVP promptly and mention any dietary needs. Irish weddings are long (ceremony, drinks reception, dinner, speeches, dancing late), so plan to stay near the venue."
     ]
    },
